Subject: Updated credit union count forecasts — what changed and what it shows

Thanks to whoever raised the Region 2 question — it made this better, and the updated workbook is attached.

Picture the credit unions in your district sorted into seven buckets by size: the smallest under $10 million in the first, the largest over $10 billion in the last. Over the years the buckets empty out, because mergers are steady and new charters are rare. But there's also a slow shuffle upward: a credit union that grows past $500 million leaves one bucket and lands in the next. When we built the first forecast, we looked at each bucket on its own — twenty-one years of history for that bucket, checked against years it had never seen — and asked what its count would look like ahead. That's a fair way to answer the question we were given, and those bucket-by-bucket pictures haven't much changed. What it couldn't see was the movement between them. Nobody turns up in the $1B–10B bucket out of thin air; they were sitting in $500M–$1B the year before. Looking at each bucket separately, we never checked that the credit unions arriving at the top had actually left the ones below. In most districts the slippage was too small to notice. In Region 2's state charters it wasn't — the upper buckets filled faster than the lower ones emptied, and the region ended up holding more credit unions than went into it. That's what your question caught.

So we've turned the problem around. We now count the whole population first — how many credit unions a region and charter type will have at all — and then work out how they spread across the seven buckets, in a way that always adds to one hundred percent of whoever is there. The shuffle upward is still fully visible; what can't happen anymore is the population growing. One figure moves as a result, and it's worth knowing before it comes up in a visit: the earlier numbers had Region 2's state charters gaining about sixteen institutions over five years, which turned out to be that accounting gap rather than real growth. They're actually headed from 523 today to roughly 464 — shrinking, but more slowly than the federal charters beside them, so they hold about 35 percent of the region either way. Holding ground on a shrinking field, not gaining. If the growth figure came up in any outside conversation, a quick word would be worth it.

With that settled, the picture the numbers paint is remarkably consistent, and it's worth carrying into your visits. Across all three regions and both charter types, the population goes from roughly 4,240 today to about 3,580 in five years — down a little over a sixth. But nearly nine-tenths of that decline sits in the two smallest buckets alone: credit unions under $50 million go from about 1,900 to about 1,320, roughly a third of them gone. The $100M–500M bucket barely moves by comparison, down about eight percent. And the $1B–10B bucket grows in every single one of the six region-and-charter groups — not one exception — taking institutions over $1 billion from about 455 today to about 530.

What that means practically is that your caseload is not just shrinking, it is changing shape. Today a little under half of the credit unions you supervise are under $50 million; in five years it's closer to a third. Institutions over $1 billion go from about one in nine to about one in seven. Fewer charters, but each one larger and more complex than the one before it — which points at examiner hours, specialty skills, and scheduling well before it points at headcount. The one-year figures are the ones to lean on for planning; the three- and five-year numbers are directional, since nobody can forecast the merger pace that far out. Ranges in the new workbook are wider than before, because they now account for both how many credit unions there'll be and how they sort themselves. Questions always welcome — this one paid for itself.